# P02 build outline

## 1. Introduction

State the three query results with the oracle qualifier in the first paragraph. Contrast a structural obstruction with a failed attempt to translate a complex quantum circuit. Give known UNIQUE-SAT as an immediate warning against 'modal computation is always classical' slogans.

## 2. Access and exact-readout model

Define finite-dimensional characteristic-two field states, pointwise fixed G0/G1 oracle, QXOR specialization, allowed fixed linear evolution, arbitrary ancillas and complete recorded instruments. Explicitly disallow full-description access as a free readout. Prove the uncertain-state-set/direct-sum discrimination lemma and explain its established linear/MQT antecedents.

## 3. One-query affine relations

Give the Deutsch four-state relation, including the w=0 identical-state case. Provide the standard-XOR two-query construction and the G0=G1 counterexample to unqualified upper bounds. Prove DJ by the three balanced supports with XOR zero. State the classical upper bound and its nonoptimality status in the modal model.

## 4. Secret-degree and exact BV

Show O_s affine in secret bits, q-query amplitude degree at most q, span dimension at most sum_{j<=q} binomial(n,j), and independent states required for exact2^n-label discrimination. Give an explicit induction covering complete adaptive histories and variable early termination up toq. Show QXOR queries at standard basis addresses attainn. Explain why this polynomial representation is not the complex-amplitude one-query BV model.

## 5. Kickback without a Fourier analyzer

In A, show X(1,R^2)=R^2(1,R^2), the Smith form of H_z, binary rank4+2n and modular character obstruction. Distinguish four-level R kickback from the standard two-level target and shared from independent literal factors. State exactly which primitive/effect assumptions matter. Do not imply all finite-field Fourier transforms fail.

## 6. Positive control and access ablations

Credit Willcock-Sabry and give one-query/O(n)-other-gates UNIQUE-SAT reproduction. Summarize, rather than headline, the finite Simon scan and conventional Grover mechanism. Show full-description readout trivially changes the access problem. Include exact finite sizes and no probability-based success rate.

## 7. Related work, limitations and open complexity questions

Compare modal, semiring and discrete-field computation to the exact current contract; identify standard polynomial/discrimination ingredients. State unresolved priority and DJ/SI generalizations. Separate field-based lower bounds from chosen ring kickback analysis.

